[?2004lVacuum Characterization of a Compact Room-temperature Trapped Ion
System
Yuhi Aikyo,1, a) Geert Vrijsen,1 Thomas W. Noel,2 Alexander Kato,3 Megan K. Ivory,4 and Jungsang Kim1, 5
1) Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Duke University, Durham, NC 27708,

USA
2) ColdQuanta, Inc., Boulder, CO 80301, USA
3) Department of Physics, University of Washington, Seattle, WA 98105, USA
4) Sandia National Laboratories, Albuquerque, New Mexico 87185, USA
5) IonQ, Inc., College Park, MD 20740, USA

arXiv:2007.07950v2 [physics.atom-ph] 8 Dec 2020

(Dated: 10 December 2020)

We present the design and vacuum performance of a compact room-temperature trapped ion system for quantum computing, consisting of a ultra-high vacuum (UHV) package, a micro-fabricated surface trap and a small form-factor ion
pump. The system is designed to maximize mechanical stability and robustness by minimizing the system size and
weight. The internal volume of the UHV package is only ≈ 2 cm3 , a significant reduction in comparison with conventional vacuum chambers used in trapped ion experiments. We demonstrate trapping of 174 Yb+ ions in this system
and characterize the vacuum level in the UHV package by monitoring both the rates of ion hopping in a double-well
potential and ion chain reordering events. The calculated pressure in this vacuum package is ≈ 2.2 × 10−11 Torr, which
